  <h2>Does the picture have to show your product?</h2>

  <p>Start here, because it settles most cases before price comes up.</p>

  <p>A stock library can hold an enormous archive, and none of it shows your SKU. A text-to-image engine has never seen your bottle, your label or your exact shade of green. It renders something plausible. Plausible is fine for a mood board and wrong for a product page.</p>

  <p>The risk isn't only aesthetic. Advertising regulators judge a picture by what a shopper takes from it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A stock photo with readable number plates added, shown as footage from the advertised dash cams, was "misleading, because it was not taken from footage from any of the advertised dash cams."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK advertising regulator ruling, June 5, 2024.</p>

  <p>The advertiser in that case had used a stock image and added detail to make the product look more capable. The same logic reaches an AI image that improves a product, as the regulator's own guidance says.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"If using pre- or post-production techniques, images should not be altered in a way which exaggerates what the product can do."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK advertising regulator guidance on misleading imagery, September 11, 2025.</p>

  <p>So the first sort is simple. Pictures with no product in them (a kitchen, a beach at dawn, hands holding a coffee cup with no brand) can come from stock or from a prompt. Pictures of the product come from a photo of the product, retouched or extended, and never invented.</p>

  <h2>What does a stock license actually buy?</h2>

  <p>Less than many buyers assume. A license is permission to use a file under conditions, and the conditions matter more than the download button suggests.</p>

  <p><strong>Royalty-free or rights-managed.</strong> Royalty-free means you pay once and reuse the image without further royalties, in any medium. It doesn't mean free. Rights-managed images are priced and limited by use, size, placement, duration and territory, so a second campaign means a second license.</p>

  <p><strong>Standard or extended.</strong> A standard license covers most marketing. It stops at products for resale where the image is the main value (a printed mug, a poster, a template) and at very large print runs. Both need an extended license, at an extra fee.</p>

  <p>Watch for the editorial-only tag, too. Those images carry no model or property release, so they can illustrate news and commentary but can't sell anything.</p>

  <p><strong>Nobody's exclusive.</strong> The library can license the same file to anyone, including the brand next to yours on the shelf. Exclusivity, where a library offers it at all, is a separate buy-out negotiated on request.</p>

  <p>If a licensed image draws a claim, the library's protection runs out at a ceiling. Some agreements also cover the file only in the form delivered, not the version you retouched.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Standard licenses cap print runs at 500,000 and cap the library's legal protection at $5,000 to $10,000; one library raises that to $250,000 per image with an extended license.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: four published stock license agreements and three plan pages, read October 12, 2026, category level, no library named.</p>

  <p>One more clause matters now that so much editing runs through AI. One large library group's agreements allow AI for permitted editing of a licensed creative image, but bar any training use, including uploading the file to an AI tool that acquires rights in it. Read that clause before you put a stock photo into any generator.</p>

  <h2>What does each route cost, and when do you pay?</h2>

  <p>Stock is cheap per image and priced up front. For a picture with no product in it, that is hard to beat.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Entry stock subscriptions start at about $29 to $30 a month, which works out to roughly $3 to $10 a standard image.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: lowest monthly plans on three stock libraries' public price pages, collected October 12, 2026; per-image figures as printed, or the monthly price divided by the images it includes.</p>

  <p>Those are the published entry plans as of October 12, 2026. Extended licenses are sold on top of the standard one, and video and premium collections cost more. Rights-managed images are quoted per use, so there's no published band to compare.</p>

  <p>Generation is priced differently: per run, not per file you keep. You pay for each attempt, including the ones you throw away, so cost follows how many tries a picture takes. Starting from a real product photo changes what can go wrong: the engine works from your product instead of inventing one, so the review checks for drift, not for a different object.</p>

