  <p>If the real comparison is a new shoot, the <a href="/resources/insights/product-photography-cost-per-sku">product photography cost per SKU piece</a> lays out how studio pricing scales. And if you already hold one clean photo of each product, that photo is the asset the third route reuses.</p>

  <h2>Who owns an AI-generated product image?</h2>

  <p>This section describes production practice, not legal advice. Ask counsel before you register or enforce anything.</p>

  <p>In the United States the starting point is settled. A machine can't be an author, and a prompt on its own doesn't make you one.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Copyright "does not extend to purely AI-generated material, or material where there is insufficient human control over the expressive elements," and "prompts do not alone provide sufficient control."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US Copyright Office, Copyright and Artificial Intelligence, Part 2: Copyrightability, January 29, 2025, executive summary.</p>

  <p>The courts agree, and the Supreme Court has declined to revisit the point.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Copyright Act "requires all eligible work to be authored in the first instance by a human being." The Supreme Court declined to hear the appeal on March 2, 2026.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US Court of Appeals for the D.C. Circuit, Thaler v. Perlmutter, No. 23-5233, March 18, 2025; Supreme Court docket No. 25-449.</p>

  <p>That isn't the end of it for product work. The same report draws a line that matters a great deal when the input is your own photograph.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">When "a human inputs their own copyrightable work and that work is perceptible in the output, they will be the author of at least that portion of the output." That copyright "would not extend to the AI-generated elements standing alone."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US Copyright Office, Part 2: Copyrightability, January 29, 2025, Section II.E, page 24.</p>

  <p>Read that against the three routes. A text-to-image picture is mostly unprotected, so a rival who copies it has little to answer for. An edit of your own photo keeps protection over the photo where it shows through, but not over the generated room around it. The photo has to stay clearly visible. The Office's Review Board upheld a refusal where a photo went in and the restyled result no longer clearly showed it.</p>

  <p>Stock gives you no copyright at all, only a license. The library or the photographer keeps it.</p>

