  <h2>Do you have to label AI-generated product images?</h2>

  <p>Sometimes, and Europe is where the rule is written down. Since August 2, 2026, Article 50 of the EU AI Act has required whoever decides how AI is used in a campaign to disclose deep fakes. And a deep fake isn't only a face: the Commission reads the "objects" in the legal definition to include consumer goods.</p>

  <p>The Commission's guidelines draw the line where this comparison does.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">An AI image of a product that can mislead "as to the actual product appearance, characteristics or use" counts as a deep fake. A real product "shown in an advertisement against an AI-generated background" does not, as long as the ad does not mislead about the product.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: European Commission guidelines on Article 50 of the AI Act, C(2026) 5054, July 20, 2026, examples after paragraph 116.</p>

  <p>The guidelines also class aesthetic background replacements and compositions of existing products in product ads as minor edits. So the third route, done honestly, usually stays out of the label. An invented product shot that looks better than the real one doesn't.</p>

  <p>A label doesn't rescue a misleading picture, either. The UK regulator's guidance says small print is "generally unlikely to prevent an ad from misleading consumers." The <a href="/resources/insights/eu-ai-act-labeling-brand-content">EU AI Act labeling piece</a> covers who applies the label, where it sits and what the platforms add.</p>

  <h2>Can an AI image keep your product accurate?</h2>

  <p>Only if it starts from the product. An edit begins with your photo and changes what you name: the background, the light, the surface, the props. The product's shape and label come from the source.</p>

  <p>Even then, check. Engines re-render pixels, and the small things drift first: the words on a label, a cap's color, the count of items in a pack. Three habits help.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Shoot or pick a sharp, evenly lit source photo with the label readable. hubStudio's Image studio resizes source pictures in your browser to 1,536 pixels on the long side before sending them, so fine print needs to be legible at that size.</li>
    <li>Protect the product. On the ChatGPT Image engines in hubStudio, an optional mask marks what may change in the first image, and everything else stays as it is.</li>
    <li>Compare every result against the real item before it ships: label, color, proportions, and anything a shopper would hold you to.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The <a href="/resources/how-to/product-photo-to-lifestyle-image">how-to on turning a product photo into a lifestyle image</a> walks through prompts and engine choice step by step.</p>

  <h2>Which route is fastest?</h2>

  <p>That depends on what slows you down.</p>

  <p>Stock is as fast as your search. When the right photo exists, it's yours in minutes. When it doesn't, you can lose an afternoon to near-misses.</p>

  <p>With generation, the wait turns into rounds. Each attempt is one run, and you can start the next before the first finishes. The slow part moves to review: checking that the scene reads right and that nothing in the product has shifted.</p>

  <p>The edit route adds one step at the start: a usable product photo. Once that exists, it runs at the speed of generation, and every review starts from the real item rather than an invented one.</p>

  <h2>How do the three routes work in the hubStudio app?</h2>

  <p>Two of the three run in the Image studio. Text to image renders from your description. Edit an image rewrites the pictures you upload following your instruction (change a background, restyle a product shot), with up to four source pictures depending on the engine.</p>

  <p>Money works the same way on both. The app runs on a prepaid balance in real currency, with no subscription. The price of a run sits next to the button before you click, and a failed run is never charged.</p>

  <p>Every render lands in History with its prompt and the engine that made it. That's the record you want when someone later asks how a picture was made, or whether it needs a label.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/app/create">Create page of the hubStudio app</a> sets out the engines and the Image studio in more detail.</p>

  <h2>When should you still choose stock?</h2>

  <p>Whenever the picture has to be a photograph of the real world. A real city skyline, a real event, a documentary scene, a news moment: generation can imitate those, and the imitation is the problem. Stock also wins when you need a generic photo in the next ten minutes and no product appears in it.</p>

