  <h2>FAQ</h2>

  <h3>Is it cheaper to use AI images or stock photos?</h3>

  <p>Per image, entry stock plans work out to roughly $3 to $10 a standard image on the price pages we read in October 2026. Generation is priced per run, so the cost depends on how many attempts a picture takes. For product content the fair comparison is with a reshoot, because neither stock nor a text prompt can show your actual product.</p>

  <h3>Can I copyright an AI-generated image?</h3>

  <p>Not the purely generated parts, in the United States. The Copyright Office says prompts alone don't give enough control, and the courts require a human author. Your own photo used as the input stays protected where it is clearly visible in the result, along with creative arrangement and edits. This is production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h3>Can I use AI-generated images commercially?</h3>

  <p>Usually, yes, subject to the terms of the tool you used and the rules where the ad runs. Two limits matter for products. The picture must not mislead about the product, whatever made it. In Europe, an AI image that could pass as the real product, and mislead, is a deep fake that needs a visible label.</p>

  <h3>Can AI show my actual product?</h3>

  <p>Only if you give it your product. A text prompt produces a plausible stand-in, not your SKU. Editing your own product photo keeps the real item and changes the scene around it. Check every result against the product, starting with the small print and the colors, which drift first. A mask, where the engine supports one, keeps the product untouched.</p>

  <h3>Are stock photos exclusive to my brand?</h3>

  <p>No. Standard stock licenses are non-exclusive, so the library can license the same file to anyone, rival brands included. At least one major library offers exclusive rights to a royalty-free image only as a separately negotiated buy-out. The library or the photographer keeps the copyright; you hold a license with conditions and a capped warranty.</p>

  <h3>Do I have to label AI-generated product images?</h3>

  <p>In the EU, a deep fake needs a visible label from August 2, 2026, and an AI product image that misleads about the product's look or use counts as one. A real product shown against an AI background, with nothing misrepresented, does not, according to the Commission's guidelines. A label never makes a misleading image acceptable.</p>
